"# Read/Write Sets and Conflict Analysis\n\nUsing the same assumed interleaving as before (timestamps t1–t13, T1/T2 overlapping from snapshot v0, T3 starting after T1 commits).\n\n## 1. Read and Write Sets\n\n| Txn | Read Set RS(T) | Write Set WS(T) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 | RS(T1) = {A, B} | WS(T1) = {A, B} | read-then-write on both items |\n| T2 | RS(T2) = {A, B} | WS(T2) = {A, B} | identical access pattern to T1 |\n| T3 | RS(T3) = {A, B} | WS(T3) = ∅ | **read-only** — no write-write conflicts possible |\n\n**Set-theoretic relationships:**\n- WS(T1) ∩ WS(T2) = {A, B} → direct **write-write collision**\n- RS(T2) ∩ WS(T1) = {A, B} and RS(T1) ∩ WS(T2) = {A, B} → bidirectional read/write overlap\n- WS(T3) = ∅ → T3 conflicts only as a reader\n\n## 2. Conflict Enumeration\n\n### Pair T1 ↔ T2 (fully overlapping, concurrent)\n\n**On item A** (T1.R(A)@t1, T2.R(A)@t2, T1.W(A)@t3, T2.W(A)@t4):\n\n| # | Operation pair | Type | Edge | Snapshot-Isolation reality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | T1.R(A) → T2.W(A) | **RW** (anti-dependency) | T1 → T2 | materialized: both read v0, T2 writes newer version |\n| 2 | T2.R(A) → T1.W(A) | **RW** (anti-dependency) | T2 → T1 | materialized: same, opposite direction ← **cycle** |\n| 3 | T1.W(A) vs T2.W(A) | **WW** | T1 → T2 (version order A₁ < A₂) | first-committer-wins: **T2 aborted here** |\n| — | T1.W(A) → T2.R(A) | WR | — | **not materialized** — T2 reads snapshot v0 (A₀), never sees A₁ |\n\n**On item B** (T1.R(B)@t5, T2.R(B)@t6, T1.W(B)@t7, T2.W(B)@t9): mirror image —\n\n| # | Operation pair | Type | Edge | SI reality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | T1.R(B) → T2.W(B) | **RW** | T1 → T2 | materialized |\n| 5 | T2.R(B) → T1.W(B) | **RW** | T2 → T1 | materialized ← **cycle** |\n| 6 | T1.W(B) vs T2.W(B) | **WW** | T1 → T2 | second abort justification for T2 |\n| — | T1.W(B) → T2.R(B) | WR | — | not materialized (T2 reads B₀ from its snapshot) |\n\n> Key SI subtlety: although T1.W(A)@t3 *precedes* T2.R(A)@t2…wait, it *follows* it — but even where a W precedes a concurrent R in real time (e.g., T1.W(B)@t7 before T2.W(B)@t9 with T2.R(B)@t6 earlier), **no WR edge forms** between concurrent writers because both read the frozen snapshot version. WR edges in SI only cross snapshot boundaries.\n\n### Pair T1 ↔ T3 (T3's snapshot opens after T1 commits @t8)\n\n| # | Operation pair | Type | Edge | SI reality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | T1.W(A)@t3 → T3.R(A)@t11 | **WR** (reads-from) | T1 → T3 | materialized: T3 reads A₁ |\n| 8 | T1.W(B)@t7 → T3.R(B)@t12 | **WR** (reads-from) | T1 → T3 | materialized: T3 reads B₁ |\n\nNo RW or WW: T3 writes nothing, and T3's reads come *after* T1's writes in version order.\n\n### Pair T2 ↔ T3\n\n| Conflicts | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| **None materialized** | T2 aborted at t10 → its writes (A₂, B₂) never entered version order; T3's snapshot (v1) contains only T1's versions. Had T2 committed before T3's snapshot, we would see WR edges T2 → T3 on A and B by symmetry. |\n\nRead-read pairs (T1.R vs T2.R, anything with T3's reads) are never conflicts.\n\n## 3. Conflict Summary Matrix\n\n| Pair | WR (reads-from) | RW (anti-dep) | WW (write-write) | Materialized total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T1 ↔ T2 | 0 (blocked by snapshots) | **4** (2 cycles: A-pair, B-pair) | **2** (A, B) | 6 |\n| T1 ↔ T3 | 2 (A, B) | 0 | 0 | 2 |\n| T2 ↔ T3 | 0 (T2 aborted) | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n\n**Conflict degree:** T1 is the hot node — it has materialized conflicts with every other transaction. T2 participates only in pre-abort conflicts; T3 only as a passive reader.\n\n## 4. What the Conflicts Decide\n\n- **WW edges (3, 6):** T1 → T2 on both items. This is the *validation evidence*: T2's write set intersects T1's committed write set → **T2 aborts** (first-committer-wins).\n- **RW edges (1–5):** Bidirectional T1 ⇄ T2 on *both* A and B → a precedence **cycle**. Had T2 committed, no serial order works (needs T1<T2 *and* T2<T1) → **lost updates**. This is also exactly the dangerous structure SSI watches for (in-and-out rw edges on one pivot).\n- **WR edges (7–8):** T1 → T3, acyclic, benign — the committed graph `T1 → T3` remains serializable.\n\n**Bottom line:** the conflict structure is maximal between T1 and T2 — every conflict type exists on every shared item in both directions — and SI resolves it by killing T2 at commit, leaving the clean reads-from chain T1 → T3."